00:07:22.445 --> 00:07:27.865
A PDP 11 memory cycle is a word, two bytes, and the low address bit picks a lane.

00:07:28.445 --> 00:07:31.565
It is not a byte address the board may store as a full location.

00:07:32.305 --> 00:07:39.065
Tom's hardware has Dave Plummer building a replacement card after period memory for the machine came with too many zeros on the tag.

00:07:39.065 --> 00:07:47.405
The card is a Q bus board, a lattice Mach-X02 FPGA is the controller, four ISSI-1 megabyte

00:07:47.405 --> 00:07:53.565
chips sit behind it, plus a U2-SIO chip, dip switches, comports, and capacitors.

00:07:53.945 --> 00:07:58.085
He tested it on a backplane next to an M8190 CPU board.

00:07:58.625 --> 00:08:05.225
That CPU board's bus is 22 bits wide, and the design reserves the top 256 kilobytes for

00:08:05.225 --> 00:08:12.045
IO and ROM. Later, PDP 11 models could hold as much as 4 megabytes. 4-1 megabyte chips

00:08:12.045 --> 00:08:16.865
are 4 megabytes of silicon, which matches that ceiling until the controller mishandles

00:08:16.865 --> 00:08:22.465
the lane bit. On a read, the CPU may want 1 byte. The controller still has to hand

00:08:22.465 --> 00:08:28.025
back both bytes. The CPU keeps the lane the low bit names. On a write, the bus still

00:08:28.025 --> 00:08:32.965
carries a whole word. If the controller stores both bytes, the lane the CPU did not mean

00:08:32.965 --> 00:08:38.625
to touch gets overwritten. Plumber's first controller returned every second word. A commenter

00:08:38.625 --> 00:08:43.625
said he had built four megabytes that turned out to be two. The chips were fine. The address

00:08:43.625 --> 00:08:48.365
decode had treated the lane bit as part of the word address, so half the array never saw

00:08:48.365 --> 00:08:55.405
a legal cycle. The repair is in the FPGA. Reads always present both bytes. Rites mask to the selected

00:08:55.405 --> 00:09:00.605
byte and leave the other byte alone. He had never flash programmed an FPGA before this board.

00:09:00.605 --> 00:09:03.645
That bring-up loop is a separate fault from the Lane Bug.

00:09:03.985 --> 00:09:08.345
A week earlier, he had already ducked the problem with a Q-bone interface carrying a

00:09:08.345 --> 00:09:12.465
Beagle-bone Black, memory implemented in software on the far side of the bus.

00:09:13.025 --> 00:09:17.825
That path cannot smash the untouched half of the word, because the Lane Select is handled

00:09:17.825 --> 00:09:19.305
in code before any store.

00:09:19.965 --> 00:09:26.165
The discrete ISSI card is the right build when the M8190 should cycle period-style chips.

00:09:26.165 --> 00:09:31.005
It stops being the right build the moment the controller cannot mask a bite lane on every

00:09:31.005 --> 00:09:31.305
right.

